-A string check answers only the first. Finding an occurrence cannot show that its context supports the attached claim ([ADR 094](../reference/adr/094-quotations-require-a-unique-occurrence-or-a-containing-range.md)), and even an exact stored copy of every claim would not establish source support ([ADR 073](../reference/adr/073-untracked-source-snapshots-require-ingest-grounding.md)). An identity check answers part of the second question (below). Context needs a reader of both texts. Wording agreement is deterministic, cheap and binary, so it can run on every pass. Judging support is costlier and noisier, so it cannot simply block the way a wording check does, and how reliably it detects out-of-context use is unmeasured.
